The attached image demonstrates Nano-11's ability to induce a transient inflammatory response and facilitate cellular uptake at the injection site. Panel A quantifies the recruitment of various immune cells, showing early neutrophil infiltration (peak 6h) followed by a rise in Mac-2+ macrophages, Ly6C+ monocytes, and antigen-presenting cells (peak Day 2), all resolving by Day 21. Panel B's confocal images confirm Nano-11 (red) is internalized by MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ cells (green), aligning with monocyte/macrophage uptake, while largely avoiding neutrophils (1A8+), thereby supporting its role in targeted antigen delivery.

Nano-11's capacity to induce a transient, localized inflammatory response and effectively target antigen-presenting cells (APCs) at the injection site significantly contributes to vaccine development. This mechanism promotes efficient antigen uptake by crucial immune cells like monocytes and macrophages, which then mature and migrate to lymph nodes to initiate adaptive immune responses. Such targeted delivery and controlled activation could lead to novel, safer, and more potent adjuvants for subunit vaccines, especially for those requiring robust cellular immunity, by optimizing the priming of T cells and antibody production.